From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rootdev: partitions of loop devices

rootdev finds the root's device by its device number under /sys/block,
looking into a disk's partitions only when they share the disk's major
number. A loop device's partitions (losetup -P, without loop.max_part)
have extended device numbers (BLOCK_EXT_MAJOR, 259), not the loop major.
NickelOS's ISO live session runs from one (its image, with writes in
memory, as a partitioned loop device), and rootdev found nothing:
chromeos_startup said "rootdev does not have stateful partition", its
mounts failed, and it "repaired" itself at every start. (loop.max_part
gives such partitions the loop major, but libbrillo's loop devices can't
be opened then.)

Now, for a root with an extended device number, loop devices' partitions
are looked into too (the SELinux policy lets cros_rootdev list a loop
device's sysfs directory: selinux-policy 0004).

---
--- a/rootdev.c
+++ b/rootdev.c
@@ -172,8 +172,14 @@
     if (depth > 5)
       break;
 
-    /* Recurse one level for devices that may have a matching partition. */
-    if (major(found_devt) == major(*dev) && minor(*dev) > minor(found_devt)) {
+    /* Recurse one level for devices that may have a matching partition.
+     * A loop device's partitions (losetup -P) have extended device numbers
+     * (BLOCK_EXT_MAJOR, 259), not the loop major, without loop.max_part:
+     * NickelOS's ISO live session runs from one. Look into loop devices
+     * for those. */
+    if ((major(found_devt) == major(*dev) && minor(*dev) > minor(found_devt)) ||
+        (depth == 0 && major(*dev) == 259 &&
+         strncmp(entry->d_name, "loop", 4) == 0)) {
       sprintf(working_path, "%s/%s", basedir, entry->d_name);
       found = match_sysfs_device(name, name_len, working_path, dev, depth + 1);
       if (found > 0)
